Friction is a powerful and versatile motion graphics application that
allows you to create vector and raster animations for web and video.
It is based on enve and uses Skia for rendering.

Friction still uses the FFmpeg 4.x API, which is not compatible with
the FFmpeg shipped with Slackware, and needs a Qt5 build of QScintilla.
As upstream does for its own Linux binaries, this SlackBuild builds a
private FFmpeg 4.4 (shared libraries in /usr/lib64/friction) and a
static QScintilla for Qt5. The bundled Skia and gperftools are built
from the Friction source tarball. They do not conflict with the system
ffmpeg or QScintilla packages.

The bundled FFmpeg enables H.264 export (libx264) only if x264 is
installed at build time (optional dependency).

Building requires clang (part of the llvm package).

By default Friction is built with GLX OpenGL integration, which works
with the NVIDIA binary driver (EGL gives a black canvas there). For the
EGL variant (upstream default, for Mesa drivers), build with:

  EGL=yes ./friction.SlackBuild
